public/
resources/
.hugo_build.lock

# `go build` in tools/<name>/ writes tools/<name>/<name>. Nothing ignored it and
# `git add -A` duly committed one: 8 887 250 bytes of Mach-O arm64, tracked
# since #16 -- the very change that added the tool.
#
# An ALLOW-LIST, not a list of names. A rule naming the binary it has already
# met goes stale at the first rename and at every new tool, and the artefact
# nobody has a rule for is exactly the one that walks in. Verify BOTH ways:
#
#     git check-ignore -v tools/versioncheck/versioncheck   # must be ignored
#     git check-ignore -v tools/versioncheck/main.go        # must print nothing
#
tools/*/*
!tools/*/*.go
!tools/*/go.mod
!tools/*/go.sum
!tools/*/testdata/
!tools/*/README.md

# The playground's modules are STAGED, never committed. `tools/wasmstage`
# writes them here during the build; the smallest is 104 KB and the largest
# 8 MB, and they move every time the factory republishes.
#
# This is a safety net, not the barrier. The barrier is that nothing in the
# repository writes into this directory: the only thing that does is a CI step,
# into a checkout that is thrown away. Verify both ways:
#
#     git check-ignore -v static/playground/modules/lua.org/lua.wasm  # ignored
#     git check-ignore -v static/playground/wasi.js                   # nothing
#
static/playground/modules/
